@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.Head
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.LearnPage
@import net.keyboardbuilders.keyboardbuilders.web.SiteUrl
@param page: LearnPage
@param head: Head
@param site: SiteUrl

<%--
    /learn/io-expander. NO BACKTICKS anywhere in this file -- one closes the raw string
    this page is written in, and jte then reports the error on the wrong line.

    SOURCES, checked 2026-10-03:
      - MCP23017 (Microchip), PCA9555 (NXP, TI) and TCA9555 (TI) are each a 16-bit I/O
        expander on the I2C bus: the makers' product pages.
      - Ferris: seed/bodies.toml, from QMK's keyboards/ferris 0_1 and 0_2 matrix.c (the far
        half scanned through an MCP23017 over I2C) and the board file (left half the chip
        and USB-C receptacle, right half the MCP23017).
      - Pando and 3w6: seed/bodies.toml, each quoting the design's own repo.
    The list at the bottom is IO_EXPANDER_EXAMPLES in Learn.kt, kept by hand. The diagram
    is a sketch, NOT to scale.
--%>
@template.layout(head = head, site = site, content = @`
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.articleLd}</script>
    <script type="application/ld+json">$unsafe{page.breadcrumbLd}</script>
    <article class="learn">
        <p class="crumb"><a href="/learn">&larr; Learn</a></p>
        <h1>IO expanders in split keyboards</h1>

        <p class="learn-answer">
            An IO expander is a small chip that gives a controller more pins. On a split
            keyboard it lets one controller run both halves: the other half gets an
            expander, which reads its keys and sends them across the cable over I2C, a
            two-wire connection between chips.
        </p>

        <figure class="learn-figure">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 152 50" role="img"
                 aria-label="Two halves of a split keyboard. The left half carries the controller chip and the USB-C port. The right half carries an IO expander chip instead. A cable between them is labeled I2C.">
                <rect class="lf-board" x="4" y="10" width="54" height="30" rx="2"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="7" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="15" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="23" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="7" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="15" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="23" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="7" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="15" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="23" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-part" x="35" y="16" width="14" height="14" rx="0.6"/>
                <text class="lf-note" x="42" y="24" text-anchor="middle">chip</text>
                <rect class="lf-sock" x="37" y="6.5" width="10" height="3.5" rx="0.8"/>
                <text class="lf-note" x="42" y="4.5" text-anchor="middle">USB-C</text>
                <rect class="lf-board" x="94" y="10" width="54" height="30" rx="2"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="122" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="130" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="138" y="13" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="122" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="130" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="138" y="21" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="122" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="130" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-cap" x="138" y="29" width="7" height="7" rx="0.8"/>
                <rect class="lf-sock" x="102" y="18" width="12" height="10" rx="0.6"/>
                <text class="lf-note" x="108" y="24" text-anchor="middle">IO exp.</text>
                <path class="lf-wire" d="M58 25C70 32 82 32 94 25"/>
                <text class="lf-label" x="76" y="27" text-anchor="middle">I2C</text>
                <text class="lf-label" x="31" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Left half: the controller chip</text>
                <text class="lf-label" x="121" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Right half: an IO expander</text>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>
                Not to scale. A Ferris-style split: the controller chip on the left, an IO
                expander on the right.
            </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Most splits have a controller in each half, talking to each other. With an
            expander there is one controller to buy, fit and flash, and the firmware sees
            one keyboard instead of two halves each running their own copy. The MCP23017,
            PCA9555 and TCA9555 used below each add 16 pins.
        </p>
        <p>
            The catch is that the halves are no longer the same. A Ferris has the chip and
            USB-C port on the left and an MCP23017 on the right, so it can't be a
            <a href="/learn/reversible-pcb">reversible PCB</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
            This list is kept by hand and surely missing some.
        </p>
        @template.learn.list(list = page.list("examples"))
    </article>
`)
